---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Answer from "../../components/Answer.astro";
import SourceLine from "../../components/SourceLine.astro";
import { dotGuideAnswer, sourceEntries } from "../../lib/answers.js";
import { catalog } from "../../lib/catalog.js";
import {
  ECFR_TIN_URL,
  FR_PREFIX_NOTE,
  FR_PREFIX_QUOTE,
  FR_TIN_URL,
  METHODOLOGY_CAVEAT_HREF,
  OLD_TO_NEW_ID,
  OLD_TO_NEW_LEAD,
  OLD_TO_NEW_NOSCRIPT,
  oldToNewCaption,
  oldToNewFileSentence,
  oldToNewReport,
  oldToNewStatus,
  oldToNewSummary,
} from "../../lib/dot-old-to-new.js";
import { article } from "../../lib/structured.js";
import { clampText } from "../../lib/text.js";

const answers = dotGuideAnswer(catalog);
const oldToNew = oldToNewReport(catalog.plants);
const description = clampText(answers.join(" "));
const jsonLd = [
  article({
    headline: "How to read a DOT tire code",
    description: answers.join(" "),
    path: "/guides/dot-code/",
    dateModified: catalog.meta.verifiedAt,
  }),
];
const crumbs = [
  { href: "/", label: "Home" },
  { href: "/guides/", label: "Guides" },
  { href: "/guides/dot-code/", label: "DOT code" },
];
---

<Base title="How to read a DOT tire code" description={description} breadcrumbs={crumbs} narrow jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h1>How to read a DOT tire code</h1>
    <Answer sentences={answers} />
    <SourceLine sources={sourceEntries(catalog.meta, ["plants"])} />
    <p>
      The tire identification number, or TIN, is molded into the sidewall. The letters DOT in front of it are a certification mark: the tire conforms to an applicable Federal Motor Vehicle Safety Standard. DOT is not the plant code.
      The rules are in <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/49/574.5">49 CFR 574.5</a>
      (<a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-49/subtitle-B/chapter-V/part-574/section-574.5">eCFR</a>).
    </p>
    <p>
      49 CFR 574.5 requires the full TIN on one sidewall. For radial tires covered by FMVSS No. 139, the other sidewall carries either the full TIN or a partial TIN that leaves off the date code, and may also leave off an optional code. Retreaded tires are excepted from that other-sidewall rule.
      See <a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-49/subtitle-B/chapter-V/part-571/subpart-B/section-571.139">49 CFR 571.139 S5.5.1</a>
      and <a href="https://www.nhtsa.gov/vehicle-safety/tires">NHTSA's TireWise page</a>.
      If a code you can read has no date, check the other sidewall.
      The date digits have their own page: <a href="/guides/tire-date-code/">how to read a tire date code</a>.
    </p>
    <h2>Current new-tire number</h2>
    <p>A new tire's TIN is 13 symbols:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Plant code, 3 symbols. NHTSA assigns it. The vPIC record used here is a company name and address, not a list of brands. <a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-49/subtitle-B/chapter-V/part-574/section-574.6">49 CFR 574.6</a> is the application for a code. The status value on that record is not a finding that the plant is operating.</li>
      <li>Manufacturer's code, 6 symbols. The maker can use it to describe the tire or the brand owner. NHTSA does not publish a legend for those symbols.</li>
      <li>Date code, 4 digits. The first two are the week, the last two are the year.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      A retreaded tire's TIN is 7 symbols: the 3-symbol plant code and the 4-digit date, with no manufacturer's code. A separate letter R marks a retread and is not part of the TIN. Matching that 7-symbol length does not, by itself, prove a tire was retreaded.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a href="/plants/">plant-code list</a> has one page per code.
      <a href="/guides/rebadged-tires/">What a DOT code can prove about who made a tire</a> says what that record does not show.
      The same records are grouped by <a href="/plants/manufacturer/">manufacturer name</a>, by <a href="/plants/country/">country</a>, and by <a href="/plants/by-maker/">maker and country</a>.
      A manufacturer group is a match on the registrant name. It is not a company's own factory list.
    </p>
    <h2 id="same-brand">Same brand, different country</h2>
    <p>
      A plant code identifies the registered plant, not the brand owner.
      A <a href="/brands/">brand page</a> lists the countries when the registrant name begins with that brand on plant records in two or more countries.
      The country name is the country on the plant record, the same label the plant page shows.
      Read the plant code, the first group of the DOT code on the sidewall, in the <a href="/decoder/">decoder</a> to see which registered plant made a given tire.
      <a href="/methodology/#data-quality">Data-quality limits</a>.
    </p>
    <h2>The date code</h2>
    <p>
      The date code is the final group. On a current number it is four digits: the week, then the year.
      The regulation's example, 0109, is the week of Sunday, January 4, 2009 through Saturday, January 10, 2009.
      A tire from before the four-digit date can show three digits, and those digits do not name the decade.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/guides/tire-date-code/">How to read a tire date code</a> diagrams that split, the Sunday–Saturday week, the April 13, 2015 length rule, where the date sits on the sidewall, and the three-digit form, including the 1990s triangle.
      This page is the rest of the number: the plant code, the manufacturer's symbols, and which characters are allowed.
      The <a href="/decoder/">decoder</a> applies the week rule to the digits you enter.
      <a href="#three-digit-dates">Three-digit dates</a> below compares one code with and without the triangle.
    </p>
    <h2 id="three-digit-dates">Three-digit dates</h2>
    <p>
      Before July 2, 2000, the date group was three symbols: the first two were the week and the third was the year, so the decade was not in the code
      (<a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-1999-07-08/html/99-17402.htm">64 FR 36807</a>).
      An isosceles right triangle molded after the number was a mark Goodyear asked to use for 1990 through 1999, and NHTSA's May 2, 1990 letter said that symbol was not prohibited
      (<a href="https://www.nhtsa.gov/interpretations/nht90-239">interpretation nht90-2.39</a>).
      <span class="code">403</span> is week 40 of a year ending in 3: with no triangle the year can be 1973, 1983, or 1993, and with the triangle the 1990s reading is 1993 while the other two years still match the digits.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>
          Worked readings of the three-symbol date 403, alone and with an isosceles right triangle after the number.
          Week dates use the Sunday-through-Saturday week in 49 CFR 574.5. The July 8, 1999 rule's examples, 3197 and 0198, fall on that same week shape.
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Symbols</th>
            <th scope="col">What this shows</th>
            <th scope="col">What this does not show</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><span class="code">403</span></th>
            <td>
              Week 40, year digit 3. The years this site lists for that digit are 1973, 1983, and 1993.
              On the Sunday-through-Saturday week in <a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-49/subtitle-B/chapter-V/part-574/section-574.5">49 CFR 574.5</a>, those weeks are October 7–13, 1973, October 2–8, 1983, and October 3–9, 1993.
            </td>
            <td>
              Which of those years was molded. The third symbol is the year and does not name the decade (64 FR 36807).
              <span class="code">403</span> is not a four-digit code for 2003. Four numerical symbols are required for tires produced or retreaded on and after July 2, 2000.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><span class="code">403</span> with an isosceles right triangle after the number</th>
            <td>
              The same week and year digit, plus the symbol in the May 2, 1990 letter.
              NHTSA told Goodyear that an isosceles right triangle after the tire identification number, meant for tires produced in 1990 through 1999, was not prohibited (interpretation nht90-2.39).
              The 1990s year ending in 3 is 1993, the week of October 3–9, 1993.
            </td>
            <td>
              That the symbol was required, or that 1973 and 1983 no longer match the digits.
              A number with no triangle is not placed before 1990 by that letter.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Some three-digit groups are also plant codes in NHTSA's list. The <a href="/decoder/">decoder</a> then reports two readings of the same symbols, a plant code and a three-digit date, and it does not combine them into one tire.
      <a href="/decoder/?tin=403">Open 403</a>
      and <a href="/decoder/?tin=403&amp;triangle=1">open 403 with the triangle</a>.
      The <a href="/guides/tire-date-code/#three-digit">date-code guide</a> diagrams the three-digit group and states why the year list runs from 1971 through 1999.
      <a href="/guides/tire-age/">How old is too old</a> quotes published positions on tire age.
      <a href="/methodology/#data-quality">Data-quality limits</a> cover what a plant record does not show.
      This table does not say whether a tire should stay in service.
    </p>
    <h2>Older two-symbol plant codes</h2>
    <p>
      Plants that already had a two-symbol code could keep using the older layout until April 13, 2025: two symbols for the plant, up to two for the size, up to four optional descriptive symbols, then the four-digit date.
      That end date is the text of 49 CFR 574.5(a)(5) and 574.5(g), adopted in the
      <a href="https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2015/04/13/2015-08418/tire-identification-and-recordkeeping">April 13, 2015 final rule</a>
      (80 FR 19553).
      Tires made before that date can still show the older code. The plant list includes a former code when NHTSA published one, and the <a href="/decoder/">decoder</a> maps it to the current three-symbol code. The <a href="/plants/">plant directory</a> searches both.
    </p>
    <p>The preamble of that rule also notes the TIN was adopted in 1971.</p>
    <h3 id={OLD_TO_NEW_ID}>How a two-symbol code maps to three</h3>
    <p>
      {OLD_TO_NEW_LEAD}
      Those lengths are <a href={ECFR_TIN_URL}>49 CFR 574.5(a)(1), (a)(2), and (b)(1)</a>, adopted in the
      <a href={FR_TIN_URL}>April 13, 2015 final rule</a>
      (80 FR 19553).
    </p>
    <p>The preamble of that rule says:</p>
    <blockquote class="quote">
      <p>{FR_PREFIX_QUOTE}</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Footnote 2 of the same preamble says:</p>
    <blockquote class="quote">
      <p>{FR_PREFIX_NOTE}</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      {oldToNewFileSentence(oldToNew)}
      <a href={METHODOLOGY_CAVEAT_HREF}>Data-quality limits</a>.
    </p>
    <div data-old-to-new>
      <div class="filter-row">
        <label>
          Former or current code
          <input type="search" data-old-query autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" />
        </label>
      </div>
      <p class="status" data-old-status data-idle={oldToNewStatus(oldToNew.count)} aria-live="polite">
        {oldToNewStatus(oldToNew.count)}
      </p>
      <noscript>
        <p>{OLD_TO_NEW_NOSCRIPT}</p>
      </noscript>
      <details class="disclosure">
        <summary>{oldToNewSummary(oldToNew.count)}</summary>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <caption>{oldToNewCaption(catalog.meta.verifiedAt, oldToNew.count)}</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Former code</th>
                <th scope="col">Current code</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {
                oldToNew.rows.map((row) => (
                  <tr data-search={`${row.oldCode} ${row.code}`.toLowerCase()}>
                    <td class="num code">{row.oldCode}</td>
                    <td class="num code">
                      <a href={row.url}>{row.code}</a>
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <h2>Symbols that are allowed</h2>
    <p>
      49 CFR 574.5 allows A, B, C, D, E, F, H, J, K, L, M, N, P, R, T, U, V, W, X, Y, and the digits 0–9. G, I, O, Q, S, and Z are not in that set. The decoder drops anything outside the set and says so, because a photo or a worn sidewall is easy to misread.
    </p>
    <h2 id="age">Age is not a safety verdict</h2>
    <blockquote class="quote">
      <p>
        As tires age, they are more prone to failure. Some vehicle and tire manufacturers recommend replacing tires that are six to 10 years old, regardless of treadwear.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      That is NHTSA's description of manufacturer recommendations, not a calculation this site can make for a particular tire. NHTSA also says a full-size spare should not be used as a replacement for worn tires except in an emergency such as a flat. You cannot see aging just by looking at the tread. The source is the aging section of
      <a href="https://www.nhtsa.gov/vehicle-safety/tires">NHTSA TireWise</a>.
      <a href="/guides/tire-age/">How old is too old</a> quotes that sentence next to other published positions.
    </p>
    <p><a class="button" href="/decoder/">Decode a tire date code</a></p>
    <p><a href="/guides/tire-date-code/">How to read a tire date code</a></p>
  </div>
</Base>

<script>
  import { initOldToNew } from "../../scripts/old-to-new.js";
  const root = document.querySelector("[data-old-to-new]");
  if (root) initOldToNew(root);
</script>
